---
description: Preview front-end changes using Storybook. Applies to *.tsx
alwaysApply: true
---

After making a front-end change, make sure it has a Storybook story of the below
format:

```tsx
// YourComponent.stories.tsx, adjacent to YourComponent.tsx
import React from 'react';
import { Meta, StoryFn } from '@storybook/react';
import YourComponent, { YourComponentProps } from './YourComponent';

const defaultProps: YourComponentProps = {};

const metadata: Meta = {
  title: 'YourComponent',
  component: YourComponent,
  args: defaultProps,
};
export default metadata;

const Template: StoryFn<YourComponentProps> = (args) => (
  <YourComponent {...args} />
);

export const Default: StoryFn<YourComponentProps> = Template.bind({});

export const SomethingElse: Story<YourComponentProps> = Template.bind({});
SomethingElse.args = { someProp: 'abc' };
```

Then, preview it using Storybook by visiting:
`http://localhost:6006/iframe.html?path=/story/{title-lowercase}--{story-name-kebab}`

Where:

- `{title-lowercase}` is the Storybook title in lowercase (e.g., `LandingPageInner` → `landingpageinner`)
- `{story-name-kebab}` is the exported story name in kebab-case (e.g., `LoggedIn` → `logged-in`)

Example: `http://localhost:6006/iframe.html?path=/story/landingpageinner--logged-in`

After making a front-end change, load the `storybook-preview` skill to start
Storybook if needed and verify the component renders as intended.
